- **Theorem EN-CURVE.** Given dim 0, I₂(N) has maximal grade t−2, so the rank-1 curve is a Cohen–Macaulay Eagon–Northcott scheme with no embedded components. I derived the closed form of its Hilbert numerator, [Π(1−s^{2t+2+r}) − s^{t+1}Π(1−s^{t+1+r})]/(1−s^{t+1}), verified symbolically for t=3..15 and against Singular at t=3,4,5. Its weighted degree is d_Γ(t) = [C(3t+1,t−1) − C(2t,t−1)]/(t+1), and L_t = 2·C(2t,t−1) + (2t+2)·d_Γ(t) is an identity: the tail length splits into a b₃-axis part and a curve part. Clause (ii) is therefore one scalar non-vanishing per weighted point of the curve, d_Γ(t) in all.
- **Theorem DOMINANT-LEADER.** Every B_r, C_r has full support, so in the prompt order all leaders are b₄-powers; the "closed form of leading terms making a subset an sop" route cannot work.

